newsgroups: comp.lang.fortran,comp.unix.osf.osf1



Greetings.

My question is: how can I make STDERR to honor fortran carriage control
information and i/o redirection at the same time?

example: consider the program below:
      program T00
c
      integer         uerr, ios, jj
      character*(80)  q0tmp
c
      uerr = 0				   ! STDERR
 9110 format ('+', a)			   ! overprint string
      do jj = 1, 5
         write (uerr, 9110) 'default STDERR'
      enddo
c
      q0tmp = '/dev/tty'
      open (unit=uerr, file=q0tmp, status='unknown', iostat=ios,
     &      carriagecontrol='FORTRAN', err=110)
      do jj = 1, 5
         write (uerr, 9110) 'redefined STDERR'
      enddo
c
  110 continue
      stop
      end

The program was compiled with the command:

  $f77 -u -cross_reference -V -C -extend_source -g2 -warn nouncalled  \
                                   -synchronous_exceptions -o t00 t00.f

I do not want to use the switch "-vms" because it would introduce more
problems. Using the Bourne shell:

	(Bourne shell) t00
	+default STDERR
	+default STDERR
	+default STDERR
	+default STDERR
	+default STDERR
	redefined STDERR

notice that the default definition of STDERR did not honor overprint (the
statement is present in the output five times). the redefinition did overprint
(only one statement is present). now:

	(Bourne shell) t00 > w.del  2>&1
	redefined STDERR

notice that only the default definition of STDERR was redirected.

So, how can I have both carriage control + i/o redirection?

Thank you,
