Kevin,
	You can try this:

at the console prompt >> type
boot -fl 0,1 to get to single user mode.  If you get a prompt then your root
and can do anything (ie change the passwd file.)

If you can get to the passwd file copy it and run crack on it.  Remove all
other passwds so crack only has to deal that just that one.  Oh and you might
want to get that crack lib too.

check the file /.rhosts and see if you can come in as root from another
machine.
(rlogin no.root.passwd.com as root from the remote host).

Check /etc/exports and see if you can mount the root filesystem on another host
and edit that passwd file as root on the remote host.

Check to see if sudo has been installed (/usr/local/bin/sudo - typcally).  If
it has become a user that can use sudo and create a root shell from there.   Or
start a program using sudo that can have subshells.

Get you installation disks out.

If you want a hand give me a yell.

Andy


