In article <ACB5D2169668107F7@bifroest.demon.co.uk> Ian_Miller@bifroest.demon.co.uk (Ian Miller) writes:
>We are using the TCP socket option of sending KeepAlives (SO_KEEPALIVE).
>However, the interval between sending the KeepAlives defaults (on most
>O/Ss) to two hours which is far too long for our purposes.  It is normally
>system wide parameter, and it should be possible to change it.  However I
>cannot work out how to do it on Alpha/OSF1.  I have done this on other O/Ss
>but I am novice on OSF1.

One way to discover if a kernel variable exists is to search the
kernel's symbol table.  I did:
	% nm -B /vmunix |grep -i keep
on my 3.0 system (I'm a little slow about upgrading!) and got
    0xfffffc0000515610 G tcp_keepidle
    0xfffffc0000515618 G tcp_keepintvl
    0xfffffc00002c4d40 T u_map_keep_on_exec
    0xfffffc00002c7070 T vm_keep_on_exec
The first two look promising, huh?  Since I have the source
code, I can tell you that tcp_keepidle is initialized to
TCPTV_KEEP_IDLE, and tcp_keepintvl is initialized to
TCPTV_KEEPINTVL.  These values are described in the header
file /usr/include/netinet/tcp/tcp_timer.h
  #define TCPTV_KEEP_IDLE (120*60*PR_SLOWHZ)    /* dflt time before probing */
  #define TCPTV_KEEPINTVL ( 75*PR_SLOWHZ)       /* default probe interval */

I suspect that you want to patch tcp_keepidle to something smaller.

-Jeff




