In article <27OCT95.11245130@swdev.si.com> tillman@swdev.si.com (Brian Tillman, x8425) writes:

   In a previous article, iglesias@draco.acs.uci.edu (Mike Iglesias) wrote:

   ->I'll second Keith's note here.  Several departments have cc:mail gateways,
   ->and they have been nothing but trouble.  The cc:mail gateway will only
   ->accept one inbound connection at a time, so any high volume mailing
   ->thru the gateway takes forever to get thru.  Stick to something like
   ->Eudora and POP, and avoid the braindamaged software that claims to gateway
   ->email.

   And our experience is that with multiple SMTP gateways running and
   MX records pointing to them, there is very little trouble at all.

If there were any trouble, you'd be the last to know.

One of the characteristics of email in general is that you're the last
one to know about the problems your site causes.

...which is not to say that your fix is not a good one.  But it only
fixes one problem.  If your gateways can all stay in sync (have the
same mapping rules), if they can generate reasonable error messages
(including the ability to distinguish between temp failures and
permanent ones, and also to include the original recipient address in
a bounced mail message), if they both bounce messages to the envelope
return address AND send replies from LAN users to the Reply-To or From
header address, if they generate correctly-formatted Internet messages
(oddly enough, this is very rare), if their address translation code
always generates valid Internet address and allows any Internet
address to be reached from the LAN, ... if all of these things are
true, then your gateways might reasonably be considered to be fit
to hook to the Internet.

Good luck.

Keith

