In <46p334$ka1@nntp5.u.washington.edu> mkkuhner@phylo.genetics.washington.edu (Mary K. Kuhner) writes:

>We are running OSF/1 on an Alphastation 400 4/233 and have been for
>some time.  We have a recurring problem with a kernel process
>that gets bigger and bigger until we're forced to reboot to salvage
>the space.  The "ps aux" entry looks like this:
>
>USER       PID %CPU %MEM   VSZ  RSS TTY      S    STARTED         TIME COMMAND
>root         0  0.0 16.6  155M  10M ??       R <    Sep 15    52:20.19 [kernel idle]
>
>This is under 2 months' growth and is getting around to the point of 
>seriously impacting system speed and swap space (we regularly get
>"only 10% swap space left" errors).  Time to reboot yet again....
>
>Why does the kernel grow like this? How can we limit its relentless growth?

You're looking at a red herring.  The virtual size of the kernel is,
on all DEC OSF/1 systems I've seen, physical memory + swap space
configured - epsilon.  This is definitely not the source of your
problems.  For example, on my system, with 64 MB RAM and 128 MB swap:

USER       PID %CPU %MEM   VSZ  RSS TTY      S    STARTED         TIME COMMAND
root         0  0.0 22.2  184M  144 ??       R <    Oct 18    51:26.18 [kernel idle]

But if I look at the usage of the swap space, I see:

    ues5:~/tmp 15> swapon -s
    Swap partition /dev/rz1b:
	Allocated space:        16384 pages (128MB)
	In-use space:            3362 pages ( 20%)
	Free space:             13022 pages ( 79%)

Plenty of free swap space (well, I use lazy swap allocation).

To spot your real problem, look for other processes which use a lot of
virtual memory.  Some of them might have memory leaks.  Killing and
restarting them on a regular basis will avoid the need to reboot the
machine.

Dan
