organization: the top of Solsbury Hill.
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

With <46p0lt$aqc@hermes.cair.du.edu>,
it seems hugh arnold (harnold@du.edu) sez:

+ I am running DU3.1+ some patches on an Alpha 2100.  About once a month and
+ a half or so, the system locks up.  To be more honest, it seems to have filled
+ its process table.  fork's are disallowed.  Generally, I keep the system
+ pretty zombie free, and I assume no single user could be responsible.  
+ About which, where do I find a list of all the kernel options.  Can I restrict
+ the amount of memory a single user can use?  The number of processes they
+ run et cetera?  


Regarding that last one:  there should definitely be a hard limit 
on the number of processes that a nonpriv'd user can run.  You can
set this in the kernel itself; go into /usr/sys/conf and set the
line:

	maxuprc         15

This, for example, would set a limit of 16 processes at any one time,
including zombies (which themselves usually get cleaned up as long as
they're wai()'ed on).


Luck++;
Phil

