The gateways I've managed have been front-ended by a Unix machine, which
then takes the responsibility of privately spoon-ffeding the gateways
behind the scenes.


But why do all this work?  Why not just use Internet Exchange from
International Messaing Associates?  I know of a number of places
(including Mitre and the US Navy in at least one installation each)
running it naked on the Internet, with no Unix box as a front-end.

In fact, I'd say that this software is probably more capable than many of
the older/less capable Unix boxes.  IDA or Version 8 sendmail can do
better when it comes to SMTP connections, but I haven't run into anything
older (or anything else, for that matter) that can say the same.

