Hi,

You should use sed or awk. You can give sed a command to edit a file. For 
example the following will substitute this for that in a file fred.txt, 
and create newfred.txt as the modified file.

# sed -e 's/this/that/g' fred.txt > newfred.txt

the /g at the end means that the substitute will occur more than once in 
a line (do a man sed for more details).

If you need to substitute a special character, you will need to use the \ 
character before it. For example the following command will substitute / 
for \ (useful for moving from UNIX to DOS file specifications!)

# sed -e 's/\//\\/g' fred.csh > fred.bat

The first \/ is the '/' character to replace it, and the \\ is the '\' 
character that replaces the '/'. This can be a bit difficult to see, but 
it is easy if you practice.

A very useful sequence to use is ^ to signify the beginning of a line, $ 
the end. The following line will replace './' with 'cp '

# sed -e 's/^\.\//cp ' fred.txt >newfred.txt

An * is a useful wildcard (There are loads more in the manual pages). The 
following example will remove from the occurence of the word 'string' to 
the end of a line.

# sed -e 's/string*$//' fred.txt >newfred.txt

I don't have a UNIX machine to hand at the moment, so I am sure to have 
got something wrong. The key thing is to try things out. You will soon 
realise who powerful regular expressions are.
Mark Jeffery
