Note that NetBSD is in progress of having an Alpha port, too. See
http://www.netbsd.org. The current stage is at least as useable as
Linux/Alpha. There are bootprom images ftp-able from ftp.digital.com
for a number of models that are known not to run Digital UNIX. Those
images allows booting for at least NetBSD and Linux. I don't know
exactly whether the Alpha models discussed here are affected by that.

NetBSD fixes many problems Linux has in a number of applications
(Linux has it's benefits, too). SMP is not in NetBSD, either, but the
benefits of SMP for operating systems that previously had light,
efficient uniprocessor kernels are not really known. There is a number
of very respected people who think the good responce behaviour (the
"light" feeling) of NetBSD, FreeBSD and Linux may be damaged by any
kernel SMP work.

