>>>>> "Ayman" == Ayman Ghanem <ayman@uiatma.atmos.uiuc.edu> writes:
Ayman> Sorry if this sounds like a basic question.  I put a crontab
Ayman> file to be run by cron from my home directory, but every time
Ayman> it runs cron uses SHELL /usr/bin/sh and PATH :/usr/bin
...

Ayman-
There are two ways to specify a shell for a script. 
1) Write the script with 
#!/usr/bin/csh
as the first line and make it executable (chmod 755 file).  The script
then becomes an executable command.  In this case, you call file as a
command from your crontab (use the full path name).

2) Explicitly call the shell (just a program) with the script file as
an argument, as in
$ csh file
($ is your prompt).  There's no reason your crontab command couldn't
be csh file.

Setting SHELL will not change the execution shell used for the
script in either case.


This newsgroup (comp.unix.osf.osf1) is for osf1-specific queries only.
A more appropriate group for this sort of query is
comp.unix.questions, and I'd appreciate it if you'd ask general
questions there.  Doing so will keep c.u.o.o focused, distribute your
question to an audience with broader expertise and thereby increase
chances of a response, and may help another user.  Thanks.

I hope the above helps.
