From: Casper.Dik@Holland.Sun.COM (Casper H.S. Dik - Network Security Engineer)
Subject: Re: What does Port 111 normally do?
Date: 1995/10/25
Message-ID: <46l5vs$4m0@sungy.Germany.Sun.COM>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 118087536
references: <46ebmf$i6n@alpha.delta.edu> <46f39m$4j6@alpha.delta.edu> <46gb7p$p7a@alpha.delta.edu>
organization: Sun Microsystems, Netherlands
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

keith@alpha.delta.edu (Keith DeLong) writes:

>But WHAT is a sun remote procedure call?

Port 111 is the portmapper in Sun RPC.  It keeps a registry of all
Sun RPC (procedure, version, protocol) -> (protocol info [usually tcp/udp
portnumber).

The first action of a Sun RPC call is to contact the portmapper and get
the address of the procedure it wants to call.

Sun RPC is a remote procedure call mechanism layered on top
of XDR.  It's the layer on top of which stuff like NFS/rusers/lockd/NIS
NIS+/mountd etc are build.

For reference see RFCs 1014 (XDR) and 1057 (RPC v 2)

Casper
