know about Ver 2.0, but in 3.2, /sbin/swapdefault is nothing but a
symbolic link to the /dev file for the swap partition.  If this is
pointing to a non-existent location, you would probably have to reload
the operating system and/or start up with genvmunix and rebuild the
kernel.  Of course, if the swap disk has gone bad it might report the
error this way.

