   > my PC users are just that. Dial in capability for mail
   > is nice but not a necessity.
   > 
   > Since our PCs are WfW MS Mail might be good on the PC.

   Microsoft Exchange Server 4.0 (next version of MS mail) can solve
   your problem. Microsoft Exchange will contain a X.400 and a SMTP
   connector in the box. The Exchange Server will be able to serve all
   your PC users and will be able to send/receive mail to the UNIX
   users. Microsoft Exchange Server is currently in beta and will be
   released during the first half of 1996.

Let's see...

+ Does it maintain header and envelope addresses separately so that
  error messages and replies each go to the correct address?
+ Does it generate MIME messages (at least for non-text mail)
  when gatewaying to SMTP and accept them as input from SMTP?
  If so, does it use standard MIME content-types when applicable?
+ Does it understand ESMTP (EHLO) and the 8BITMIME extension?
+ Does it allow Microsoft Mail users to reply to the correct 822 addresses
  (Reply-To if present, otherwise From)?  If Reply-To or From contain
  multiple addresses, will the reply go to all of them?
+ Does it generate meaningful error messages that include the failed
  address in the format used by the sender, and a recognizable
  reason for failure?
+ Can the gateway check the validity of a recipient on receipt of
  an SMTP RCPT command and return 4xx (for temporary errors) or 5xx
  (for permanent ones) as appropriate?
+ Does it generate correctly-formatted bounced mail (NULL return path,
  sent to the envelope return address)?  (NOTARY would be even better)
+ How many inbound SMTP connections can it handle at a time?

I'd want to know all of these things and more before spending money on
a gateway to SMTP.

