If you're running IRIX 5.3, it's almost certainly worth calling SGI and
getting their latest mega-NFS rollout patch.  There are a LOT of
bug-fixes in recent versions of this.

> 4. A more detailed explanation of the svc_maxdupreqs variable. Does
>    lowering the number of cached NFS requests force the NFS server
>    to write out its changes more frequently, or does it simply cause
>    the server to encounter buffer overflows? A more detailed 
>    explanation of kernel-level NFS stuff would be most helpful.

I had a short discussion about this a while back, and (if my
recollection is right) this is the deal: svc_maxdupreqs is not a
performance thing, but instead deals with the NFS idempotency cache.
The NFS protocol is supposed to guarantee that if a client doesn't
hear back from a server for a while and resends a request such as a
write or a link, that that request doesn't get executed twice.  The
way that this is "guaranteed" is that the server remembers the last X
requests that it has received, and if it receives a duplicate, it
throws it away.  svc_maxdupreqs is the size of this cache.  If the SGI
kernel notices that things are rolling out of the cache (what it
considers) too quickly, it spits a warning to the console suggesting
to you that you the up the size.
-- 
