In article <468fe4$hlk@lll-winken.llnl.gov> oberman@icaen.llnl.gov writes:
>This may not vbe what you are looking for, but page size on Alpha
>processors, and the software running on them, is hardware dependent
>with the archetecture spec allowing page size from (I think) 4KB to
>64KB. I know OpenVMS will support any page size in this range and I
>believe Digital UNIX will do so, as well.
>
>But it's not something you can change except for buying a processor
>with a larger page size. I'm not aware of any Alpha which
>implements a page size > 4K at this time, but I don't always check
>all of the details on new processors.

This is a little confused.  The smallest page size supported,
as far as I can tell from the "Alpha Architecture Reference
Manual", is 8K bytes.  I'm not sure I entirely understand the
spec on this point, but it looks like hardware can optionally
support 16KB, 32KB, or 64KB pages.

The hardware supports "granularity hints", essentially a way to use
one TB entry to map more than one page.  It looks like the spec allows
groups of 8, 64, or 512 pages (subject to various alignment
restrictions).  Sometimes people call these "super pages".

I do NOT believe that Digital UNIX generally uses granularity
hints to save TB entries (and reduce the TB fault rate), although
they may be used in some specific cases for supporting frame buffers,
etc.  I don't know what OpenVMS or NT do with this.

I have seen at least one PhD thesis on the topic of super pages,
and I suspect there is enough work for several more.  It is not
at all obvious how to get an operating system to use them automatically.

-Jeff



