Denis@CRBM.cnrs-mop.fr (Denis Pugnere) wrote:
>
>	I have to connect an Alpha AXP workstartion to an subnetwork
>connected over an ISDN line. In order to save $$$, I would like to know 
>some things about the network activity generated by unix daemons. If some
>of them are allways discussing with other hosts over internet, the ISDN
>line will be opened all the time.

the first issue is whether you are using the Digital ISDN sw 
(supports EuroISDN not the French VG series) from Digital
or you are using an ISDN router.

Most of the non specific traffic on a net
is routed/gated or broadcast based.
But there is a whole lot of lesser daemons
that send "regular" information around to
poll or be polled.

If you are using the Digital ISDN sw
dont worry about it. As the link is
set up for POINTTOPOINT the routing
code wont send any of the "junk" 
such as route discovery or
broadcasts down the link. You can also
set timeouts for the link so that
after a preset idle time the link
collapses.

If you are using an ISDN router 
it will probably have some form
of spoofing and either save all
the packets for "burst" fowarding
or drop them altogether. You will
probably have to set the specifics
of what you want passed i.e. burst
forward Vs immediate forward Vs
dropped etc when you install the router.

>
>	Do you know which unix daemons which need to do that ?
>
>	May be sendmail, timed, timeslave, named, inetd ?

As above most "extraeneous" tarffic is route discovery
or broadcast based. As to the others they are mostly
event driven i.e. they suck on a socket waiting for
an event and are not proactive or if they are they
are infrequent. One way to have a look at this is 
to enable the packet filters on a machine and, using
tcpdump, collect statistics on say a night over a weekend
and have a look at the sorts of packets which flow
around as daemon initiated events.


>
>Many thanks for your help.
>
>

Jon

