In Article <45he5q$769@news.nic.com>
bbp@nic.com (Bruce B. Platt) writes:
>What's the best way to move a complete set of file systems to a new disk?
>
>Is the best way to do this to dump to tape, make newfs on the new disk, 
>then restore from tape,  Or is there is way to bypass the dumping and 
>restoring from tape?

Yes. dump to stdout and pipe it to restore. The restore man page has an
example. See the -f option.

  -f dump_file
            When an argument follows the -f modifier flag, it is used as the
            name of the archive device, replacing the default tape device
            /dev/rmt0h.  When the argument is the character - (dash), restore
            or rrestore reads from standard input.  Thus, dump and restore or
            rdump and rrestore may be used in a pipeline expression to copy
            file systems with the following typical command:
                 dump  -0f  -  /usr  |  (cd /mnt;  restore  -xf  -)

