> I have a guess at what is happening: the server is trying to get
> the host name of the client.

You're 100% correct.

> If the DNS configuration on your server is wrong...

I wouldn't take any garanties for the correct configuration of
our DNS :-( but it's a fact that a great many clients accessing these
services are not covered by DNS, i.e. their names can't be reversely
resolved.

> But it's possible that your server is configured to try more than one
> name server, and it fails on the first one and succeeds on the second
> (or third, ...).

Yes.  Local machines not covered by DNS don't cause any delays.

> One suggestion: I recommend configuring your http server to log
> IP addresses, not host names.

I did this and it works great.  I guess you can't do something
similar for telnet(1), maybe besides disabling DNS at all.
(Not that it matters, this is sort of a firewall machine where
people are not supposed to log in from outside...)

Thanks a lot for your help!


