We started using taskbroker. But taskbroker is
commercial. We changed to NQS to be platform independent.
Unfortunately NQS seemed able to handle just about
any situation but ours. We used it for about a year.
Then it suddenly stopped and I could not make it work
again. (Sounds pathetic, I know ;-)
  So I wrote my own system, WQS, in three weeks time.
WQS is designed to do exactly what I want it to do
and absolutely nothing more. It is specialized in fair
scheduling of jobs. i.e. jobs are started in such a way
as to give all active users the same amount of cpu time.

So if you have the same situation as I had, you might be
interested in running WQS. (It's free of course)

 There are always queued jobs. And angry users
 constantly ask you why their jobs have not been
 started yet. (Fair scheduling)

 The computers are not used to run parallel jobs.
 (No support whatsoever)

 There are no users who will get violent if there
 is a nice19 job running in the background of their
 workstation. (WQS never looks for an especially
 idle machine. It just takes the first in the list)

 You want something simple to manage. (WQS is simple
 to administrate just because it has not got a lot
 of functionality. A bit like cheating, admittedly)

