I have moved filesystem around frequently.  So we have a nice script using
gtar:
It will NOT cross mount points.  Be sure to specify FULL pathnames for the
directories.  For example copy /usr to a temp disk mounted on /mnt:
cpdir /usr /mnt
--
#!/bin/sh
#
# Shell script to recursively copy the contents of the FROMDIR directory
# to the TODIR directory without changing the permissions and ownership
# and not crossing filesystems in the FROMDIR directory.
#
FROMDIR=$1
TODIR=$2

echo  "Recursively copy the contents of the directory ${FROMDIR}"
echo -n "        to the directory ${TODIR} (y/n)? "
read ANS
case ${ANS} in
    [yY]*)
          echo -n "Copying... "
          cd ${FROMDIR}; /usr/local/bin/gtar clf - . | (cd ${TODIR};
/usr/local/
bin/gtar xpf -)
          echo " Done."
          ;;
    *)
          echo "Directory copy aborted by user."
          ;;

esac

-- 
Andy Sheaff - sheaff@eece.maine.edu
http://www.eece.maine.edu/Faculty/Sheaff.html




