In article <DG9yJn.C7o@cyf-kr.edu.pl>, grzes@twins.pk.edu.pl (Grzegorz Mucha) writes:
|> Hi,
|> 
|> I've compiled g77 0.5.16 using gcc 2.7.0 on DEC Alpha running dUNIX 3.2.
|> Everything seemed to be OK, but that's all I get during compilation:
|> 
|> (73)|alpha:~/FORTRAN> g77 aqq.f
|> collect2: ld returned 1 exit status
|> /usr/ucb/ld:
|> Object file format error in: /usr/local/lib/gcc-lib/alpha-dec-osf3.2/2.7.0/libf2c.a(main.o): bad isym in proc table entry
|> 
|> aqq.f is as follows:
|> 
|>        program aqq
|>        write(*,*)'Hello world'
|>        end
|> 
|> Any idea?

Yup, I gpot through this one :)

You have to modify the Makefiles (or more precisely Makefile.in) in
<wherever-your-gcc-is>/f/runtime/{libF77,libI77}

Comment out the last 2 lines in the .c.o rule
.c.o:
        $(CC) -c -DSkip_f2c_Undefs $(ALL_CFLAGS) $(CGFLAGS) $<
##      ld -r -x -o $@x $@
##      mv $@x $@

I don't know why it doesn't work that way, but that fixed it for me,
and Dave Love agrees that these 2 lines shouldn't be there.

Hope that helps,

Michel

