	I've been trying to get an older Archive Python 4mm DAT drive to work
on our AlphaStation 250 4/266 running dUnix 3.2.  I've engineered a kludge
that works, but I can't figure out why it doesn't work straight off.  

	When booting the kernel, the cam error logger indicates that an
error occured on the drive and that the SCSI bus was reset.  If I try
and use the drive at this point (tar, dump, etc.) the process hangs and
the busy light on the drive never turns on.  However, if I use <scu> to
perform a self-test diagnostics on the drive, the test passes and the drive
works happily thereafter.  My guess is that the error conditition noted
on boot up prevents the drive from working until the condition is cleared 
by the self test diagnostic.  BUT I can't figure out what's causing the
kernel to produce an error on the drive.  I've tried playing around a 
bit with the cam_data.c file, but haven't found any magic combination that 
works.  I was surprised to see that the drive is treated as a 9 track
tape drive (default), but things DO work after the self-test and changing it
to an RDAT device (which I THOUGHT would be more appropriate) breaks the fix.

	Anyway, if anyone else has encountered this problem and knows of
a more suitable fix, I'd greatly appreciate the info.
	
