B
: >Curiosity - why aren't you using cc -migrate or cc ? c89 is loaded for the
: >older C++ compilers only because of compatibility issues.

: Huh?  As far as I can tell, c89 is simply a different front end to exactly
: the same compiler.  The main difference between cc and c89 is that one
: invokes /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/cfe with the -std0 option (extended K&R) and
: the other with -std1 (ANSI).  What am I missing?

: The one which is loaded for the C++ compiler is named oldc89.

My partial mistake. DEC C++ previously used the c89 compiler - it no longer 
does (as of V 5.0). c89 is now a front end to the cc command for compatibility
with older makefiles using the c89 command. oldc89 is a very old C compiler
that DEC will not support in the future - they recommend using cc -migrate.
This is from the release notes in /usr/ccs/lib/cmplrs/cxx, where there are
more gory details.
