In article <45he5q$769@news.nic.com>, Bruce B. Platt <bbp@nic.com> wrote:
>What's the best way to move a complete set of file systems to a new disk?
>
>Specifically, the old disk has "a" and "g" partitions holding "/" and "/usr"
>respectively.
>
>I'd like to take this disk (which is actually new with factory installed 
>sw from Digital) and before I do anything else with the system, move it's 
>entire contents to a larger disk, and then use the larger disk in place 
>of the original one.

Connect new disk to machine

use disklabel to partition it as required.
newfs on a and g partitions
cd /tmp
mkdir newroot
mount /dev/rz?a /tmp/newroot
mkdir newusr
mount /dev/rz?g /tmp/newusr
dump 0f - / | (cd /tmp/newroot; restore -rf - )
dump 0f - /usr | (cd /tmp/newusr; restore -rf - )

either

shut machine down and swap SCSI ID's of the two disks
reboot

or

edit /tmp/newroot/etc/fstab to be reflect new disk ID
change the link /sbin/swapdefault to point to the new disk
shutdown
change boot varaible at the console prompt
boot

Good luck

I have just done it twice. Once from internal disk onto a spare disk
then repartitioned the internal disk to give 512Mbyte swap and then
moved / and /usr back.

Tim.


