Has anyone encountered the following problem?

I'm running OSF/1 ver. 3.2 in an DEC 3000 S 600 machine and have 
recently tried to set user quotas for our 6500 users. 

Due to such high amount of users, defining a personal quota for 
everyone is naturally out of question. So I decided to make a proto user 
as explained in the edquota manpage and then ran a script making a 
quota.user entry for others using this proto.user.

The problem is that quotas for other users aren't updated "on the fly", 
only the proto user's quota is. Still it seems that the system has noted 
those other quotas for it starts the grace period as it normally should 
when a user exceeds the soft limit and disables the user's file creation
 when the hard quota is reached. Though, when the user deletes files from 
his/hers homedirectory, the quota will not be updated, instead the grace 
time keeps on shrinking! With the proto user everything works as it should, 
even though the quota.user entries are similar.

I tried to bring the machine down and redo the quota setting process, but 
--> did not affect

I ran the quotacheck when in single user mode
---> that updated the quotas for every user, but as the manpage says 
(or as I understood it) checkquota should not be run when the disk in 
concern is in use (our machine can't be taken down to often)

Heres the fstab entry for my machine:

root_domain#root        /       advfs rw 0 0
/proc   /proc   procfs rw 0 0
usr_domain#usr  /usr   advfs rw 0 0
/dev/rz0d       swap1   ufs sw 0 2
/dev/rz10c      /ky     ufs rq,userquota 1 2
/dev/rz1a       /scratch ufs rw 1 2

--> where /dev/rz10c is the problem partition (the only one with quotas 
enabled in the system)


Could anybody comment on this, I'm running out of reference to RTFM to 
and those quotas are really needed in the system.

