htchapma@vela.acs.oakland.edu (H. Todd Chapman) wrote:

>My many bad experiences with Pathworks has made me
>seriously consider using Samba on my server instead and
>using WfW 3.11 for my clients. Our LAN consists of about
>10 Unix workstations and 30 PCs.

>This solution has left me with one big problem:
>The rest of the LANs on our WAN primarily use
>the mail client that comes with Pathworks,
>DECNET as a protocol, and VMS servers. If I am
>not mistaken I think that this means their
>mail follows the Mail-11 protocol. Correct me if
>I am misunderstanding.
>Some of our locations are using Teamlinks.

>Since I want seemless mail with the rest of our company
>what should I do?
[snip]

A low cost bailing wire and spit method:

You may wish to take a look at using -

1) Hunter Goatley's MX 4.1 mail exchanger   (available in the MADGOAT
archive)
2)  Indiana University IUPOP3  POP server for VMS.

In combination, the two packages should allow you to get MAIL-11 <->
SMTP or whatever-else-you-can-find-for-a-transport  integration.
Assumes you have a common protocol between your VMS server and your
Unix box.  

This solution won't work with WFW MS-MAIL unless you have a MS-MAIL
SMTP gate and the full MS-MAIL PO and/or Digital's Message router and
MS-MAIL gateway.   

It will work with the WIN95 exchange mailbox although headers and
routing information may look a little bit "messy".   For that matter
it will work with any standard PC mail package with SMTP/POP
access.


Hopes this helps,

Doug Coleman
