Subject: Re: Integrating Sendmail, MS-Mail and VMS Mail ???
Date: 1995/10/18
Message-ID: <MOORE.95Oct18134423@wilma.cs.utk.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 117731915
references: <45u1fd$v8q@vela.acs.oakland.edu>
organization: University of Tennessee CS Dept Primate Research Lab
newsgroups: comp.mail.sendmail,comp.mail.mh,comp.unix.ultrix,vmsnet.networks.desktop.pathworks,comp.unix.osf.osf1,vmsnet.networks.tcp-ip.misc,comp.os.ms-windows.networking.misc,comp.sys.dec,comp.mail.smail,comp.mail.misc

In article <45u1fd$v8q@vela.acs.oakland.edu> htchapma@vela.acs.oakland.edu (H. Todd Chapman) writes:

   My many bad experiences with Pathworks has made me
   seriously consider using Samba on my server instead and
   using WfW 3.11 for my clients. Our LAN consists of about
   10 Unix workstations and 30 PCs.

   This solution has left me with one big problem:
   The rest of the LANs on our WAN primarily use
   the mail client that comes with Pathworks,
   DECNET as a protocol, and VMS servers. If I am
   not mistaken I think that this means their
   mail follows the Mail-11 protocol. Correct me if
   I am misunderstanding.
   Some of our locations are using Teamlinks.

   Since I want seemless mail with the rest of our company
   what should I do? My Unix users go both ways (will
   want to access mail from unix or PC) and many of
   my PC users are just that. Dial in capability for mail
   is nice but not a necessity.

   Since our PCs are WfW MS Mail might be good on the PC.

   Is MailWorks the answer? Do I want to integrate MailWorks
   with Sendmail? Do I obviouslynot know the subject matter
   and am heading in the wrong direction? What client should I use
   on the PC (I think there are probably many free ones
   but should I pay for a comercial package?)?

Best available solution: install PMDF on your VMS system and use it as
a gateway between SMTP, your Dos/Windows LAN stuff, and Mail-11.  It
does the best job of anything at tying broken mail systems together.

Best low-cost solution: standardize on SMTP for all inter-system mail.
Pick a good interface between Mail-11 and SMTP (MX comes to mind) and
teach your VMS users to use that instead of DECnet.  Either buy an
SMTP gateway for your LAN mail systems, or install Eudora on your PCs
and let them speak SMTP and POP to your mail server.  Use a UNIX
system and sendmail as a mail hub.

FWIW: My experience is that the SMTP gateways from the vendors of LAN
mail systems uniformly suck.  They're probably reliable enough for
local use, but you should avoid them like the plague if you care about
having your PCs communicate effectively with the Internet.  If you
have the luxury of changing PC mail systems, it's far better to pick a
system that conforms to Internet standards than to try to take a
broken LAN system and make it work with a gateway.

Keith Moore

