originator: harris@juggl7.zk3.dec.com


In article <45ucgt$b65@chaos.dac.neu.edu>, johnson@neu.edu (Chris Johnson) writes:
>      I seem to remember that there was an AdvFs equivalent for the UFS
> inode in Digital UNIX (osf/1).  Further, just as with inodes, you need 
> more than the default if you have lots of small files.  So far so good.  
> I don't remember what these things are called or where you define them.  

    I think you are asking about the -x option described in the release
    notes for the mkfdmn and addvol AdvFS commands.

mkfdmn [-o] [-r] [-l log-pages] [-t type] [-x meta-extent-size] special domain

    UFS inodes hold file system metadata (information which describes the
    file and where it is stored on the disk).  AdvFS keeps the file
    metadata in the "Bitfile Metadata Table" (BMT).  The BMT can be expanded
    on demand, however, it has a limit on the number of extents it can have.
    The -x option specifies that the BMT should be extented in larger chunks
    so that more metadata can be stored in each extent (which translates to
    more small files that can be created).

    By default the BMT extent size is 128 pages (8K bytes/page).

    It does not hurt to use a larger value for -x since the extents are only
    allocated when they are needed vs the UFS preallocation of all the
    inodes in advance.

    I would suggest selecting a value of -x which is proprotional to the
    projected size of your domain.

