Subject: Re: Deleting files with odd names
Date: 1995/10/06
Message-ID: <452pde$rdd@mercury.hgmp.mrc.ac.uk>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 117148361
references: <44rk7q$bg2@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk>
content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: Genome Research
x-url: news:44rk7q$bg2@lyra.csx.cam.ac.uk
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1
x-mailer: Mozilla 1.1N (X11; I; OSF1 V3.2 alpha)

*sigh*

Of _course_ I tried '\' to escape the '/' characters!

And of course I tried wrapping quotes around the thing!

I have now fixed the problem, here's the answer that came from Burkhard
Neidecker-Lutz at Digital:

This is a known bug with old versions of MacIntosh NFS. The catch here is
that there is *NO* way to delete that file via name from UNIX, because
the kernel "nametoi" function cannot get past the "/" characters. They
can be introduced only via NFS (which doesn't use the nametoi function
but rather constructs pathes one by one).

Solution 1:

	Delete from the Mac. It created the invalid thing in the
	first place and can work around the problem.

Solution 2:

	ls -i 
	4711 name/with/slash/in/it

(the actual number will not be 4711). Unmount the file system. Now (assuming
here that the file systen is a UFS file system on disk /dev/rrz3g):

	fsdb -w /dev/rrz3g
	> 4711:inode
	> :ln=0
	> :quit

This will set the link count of the file to zero. Now force a file system
check on that file system:

	fsck -o /dev/rrz3g

and answer "yes" when fsck offers to remove the file in question.


Many thanks to all those that mailed,


	Dave




