About 2 months ago, I posted a inquiry about upgrading memory on a
DEC3000-300 with standard PC SIMMs.  The concensus seemed to be that PC
SIMMs wouldn't work 'cause (1) they were incompatible or (2) they
wouldn't perform well enough.  There were a lot of references to Kingston
memory products. 

Anyways I bit the bullet and ordered two PC industry standard 8MB, 60ns,
parity (2x36) SIMMs from Micron Technologies since they had a 30 day
money back guarantee if the SIMMs didn't work for me.  This brought my
workstation memory up to 48MB (from 32MB).  I was mistakenly sent 70ns
SIMMs which I have been using in the DECstation (with no problems at all)
while I wait for my replacement 60ns SIMMs to arrive. 

When I inquired into the prices of Kingston memory, I received quotes
ranging from $800 to over $1000.  The price for the Micron memory was
$628 (i.e. $314 for 8MB).  So, unless my workstation dies in the next 
few days, I'm pretty confident that you don't have to pay an arm and a 
leg for DEC workstation memory.  Just make sure it's 70ns or faster and 
has true parity.

- Jimmy

