I don't think Purify is available, but if you install the ATOM subset
on the OSF/1 CDrom, you can use the '3rd' (third degree) program,
which does pretty much everything Purify does, and it's free. Only
runs on Alpha, though.

Man page follows (this is actually for a more recent version than
you're likely to find on a CDrom).

third(5)                                                             third(5)



NAME

  third - Performs memory access checks and detects memory leaks

SYNOPSIS

  atom application_program  -tool third [-env environment] [-toolargs=arg1,
  arg2,...]  [atom_flags...]

PARAMETERS

  application_program
            File name of a fully linked shared or nonshared executable to be
            examined.

FLAGS

  -tool third
      Identifies the Third Degree tool to atom.

  -env environment
      Identifies any special environment.  You can invoke the Third Degree
      tool for a threaded application program by specifying the threads
      environment.  You can invoke third for a general-purpose environment by
      omitting the -env flag.

  -toolargs=arg1, arg2,...
      Passes arguments to the Third Degree tool's instrumentation routine.
      The instrumentation routine take (int argc, char **argv) as arguments.

  atom_flags
      Specifies flags to the atom command.  See the atom(1) reference page
      for descriptions of other flags accepted by the atom command.

DESCRIPTION

  The Third Degree tool performs memory access checks and memory leak detec-
  tion of C and C++ programs at run-time. The instrumented application is
  larger than the original application, runs exactly like it --just slower--
  and, in addition, logs all errors and requested reports.

  This instrumented program locates most occurrences of the worst types of
  bugs in C and C++ programs: array overflows, memory smashing, and errors in
  the use of malloc and free. It also helps you determine the allocation
  habits of your application by listing the heap and finding memory leaks.

  For example, using the Third Degree tool, a program trying to free the same
  object twice generates the following output:

       ---------------------------------------- pid=3878 --- fof ---- 3 --
       Freeing already freed memory in heap at 0x140038010
         at byte 0 of 32-byte block at 0x140038010
         - main                                         ex.c, line 21

       This block was allocated by malloc at
         - Booboo                                       ex.c, line 10
         - main                                         ex.c, line 20

       This block was freed at
         - Booboo                                       ex.c, line 14
