I just finished installing version 3.2C of OSF/1 (sorry, I will continue
to refuse to call it "Digital UNIX" until somebody else starts selling
"Analog UNIX" :-) on our almost-brand-new AlphaServer 2100 5/250 (2 CPUs,
768 Mb of memory).

Now, after two days of operation with 3.2C (following two months or so
with 3.2B, but I digress :-), I have the following questions:

1) Has anyone else noticed that the vdump/vrestore combination, on
   AdvFS file systems, doesn't appear to restore sparse files correctly?
   In my experience, all holes seem to be completely filled, which IMHO
   is definitely not a Good Thing(tm). :-(

   Am I missing something?  (Please try to refrain from too much
   gratuitous sarcasm. :-)

   If so, what?

2) I'm really confused about pty management.  Our 3.2B system hummed
   along (when it wasn't crashing, but I digress again :-) with a full
   complement of 3162 pty devices, with the usual kernel configuration
   entry of
                   pseudo-device      rpty    3162

   The release notes for 3.2C state that this entry must be replaced by

                   options            RPTY

   However, they also make the following claim (section 4.2, page 4-7):

      "See the _System_Tuning_and_Performance_Management_ manual for
       information on how to configure the number of rptys, SLIP
       lines, and the number of concurrent accesses through SNMPINFO."

   My problem?  Well, I don't have a paper copy of the 3.2C version of
   the System Tuning manual -- and the online version on the 3.2C
   documentation CD doesn't even *mention* "the number of rptys". :-(

   (I'm happy enough to RTFM, but in this case TFM doesn't seem to
   have the information I need...  or am I just reading the wrong one?)

   Now, I probably don't really need a full 3162 of them (even though
   I have created that many devices), but I definitely need more than
   whatever the default is likely to be...   Help!!

3) Where Have All the Patches Gone?

   Our campus support agreement provides us with access to patches on
   ftp.service.digital.com.

   One of the first things I did when we obtained our new machine was
   to look there for patches against 3.2B.  There was only one, which
   didn't address anything we use.  I happily assumed that this meant
   that all known patches from 3.2 and/or 3.2A had been rolled in to
   3.2B.

   Well, I was wrong.  I lost count of the number of crashes we
   experienced (3.2B is *extremely* fragile for SMP systems :-()
   before we finally gave up and, on the advice of DEC technical
   support, installed 3.2C.  Before giving us this advice, however,
   they sent us several patches -- but none of the patches we
   received, via email, were available on the patch server.

   The punch line is even better, though -- *every* *one* of the
   patches in question had an identical corresponding patch for
   version 3.2, and the 3.2 versions *are* on the patch server
   (and have been since July)!  (In particular, as part of the
   collection in ftp.service.digital.com:/osf/v3.2/osfgrp_e01032.tar.)

   [ I found it particularly poignant that one of these patches
     (namely lockprim.o -- the patch id was OSF320-036 for the 3.2
     version, but I don't know the corresponding number for 3.2B),
     has the following statement in its documentation (verbatim,
     including the capitalization):

        "This patch is MANDATORY for all multi-processor platforms."

     Nice little irony, isn't it?  It's bad enough that this wasn't
     already incorporated into 3.2B, worse still that it wasn't even
     mentioned in the release notes...  and IMHO absolutely unforgivable
     that it wasn't on the patch server for me to find *before* many
     of my users lost 12 hours of work due to an AdvFS failure caused
     by a crash which was in turn caused by not having this patch
     installed. 

     I apologize if I sound bitter, but I guess I am. ]

   Now I find that there are no patches on the server for 3.2C...

   I know that 3.2C *does* have most (all?) 3.2B patches incorporated,
   but I certainly hope that any future patches will make their way
   onto ftp.service.digital.com.

That's all the questions I have right now.  Sorry about the rant, but
I feel much better now. :-)

Please email or post (yes, I do read this group regularly :-).

Thanks in advance!

    - Steven
