In article <44pi3o$b8n@hermes.acs.unt.edu>,
Steve Tate <srt@zaphod.csci.unt.edu> wrote:
>Cheryl Marquis (cmarqui@relay.nswc.navy.mil) wrote:
>> Ummm, the easy way to hush this beep is (if you have a session
>> manager) to go into the Options menu, select "keyboard" and in the
>> window, at the top, you'll see something referencing the beeps and a
>> range bar for loud and soft.

We have a number of different vendor's workstations here (DEC Alphas &
DECstations, Suns, HPs, PCs running BSDI, etc), so I avoid proprietary
things like Digital's session manager that will only run on one
platform.  (BTW: Kudos to Digital to providing the best out-of-the-box
X11 setup of all major HW vendors). The 'standard' way to change the
characteristics of the workstation's beep is the command 
'xset b <%volume pitch duration>' which is the only command I've ever
tried to use to change the bell characteristics.  This command works
well on 3000 series Alphas and on most other machine's that I've tried
it on.  On AlphaStations, I can change the pitch & duration slightly,
but the volume is an all-or-nothing type of thing.

>I tried that due to an earlier posting suggesting exactly this.
>Unfortunately, I found the "volume" control mostly useless.  I don't
>hear any difference between volume 1 and volume 100 --- volume 0 turns
>it completely off, but I don't necessarily want that either...  Is it
>really an all or nothing deal (and I agree with Andrew Gallatin's
>posting --- it's just too damn loud)?  For those who suggested
>plugging the speaker directly into the main board, will this give me
>any kind of volume control?

Good.  I'm glad its broken under the sesssion manager too.  It looks
like something lower down than xset is broken, meaning when/if its
fixed, then xset should be fixed too.

Hopefully, plugging the speaker into the motherboard would take the
darned sound card out of the loop, and make it behave as well as other
machine w/respect to bell volume control.

Drew
