stern@phys.cwru.edu (Michael Sternberg) wrote:
>johnny@indigo.co.il (Johnny Nadir) writes:
>>We have HP printers with jetcards connected to Sun and HP unix machines. 
>>HP provides the "Jetadmin" software for them, but none for alpha(osf1) 
>>that we recently added.
>
>First you need to assign an IP-address along with a hostname to the
>printer through your local hostmaster as you would with a workstation.
>Then go through the printer's menu and see that you can type in the address
>from below the MIO menu.  An alternative is to use bootp.  You find
>the ethernet address on the test page of the printer.  If you can then
>ping the printer you are set.  It acts as a BSD lpd-spooler.  You can
>set up a printer in /etc/printcap with the remote system (rm=) being
>the DNS name of the printer and the remote printer (rp=) being "raw".
>
>You can set further options of the printer by telnetting to it.  [Yes,
>telnet - it's pretty smart].  Most of the HP-provided jetadmin software
>is toys or bells and whistles.  Check out the following for more details:
>ftp://ftp-boi.external.hp.com/pub/networking/software/
>

There are JetDirect cards out there whithout the spooling-feature!

In anycase, if you have one printer and several workstations printing
to it, you might want to do the spooling thru just one of the workstations.
This way you'll have to check only one admin-file to check who is using
the printer.
e.g., print from the HP-workstation only and use the remote-printing setup
for all the other workstations from which you want to print.

werner




